# `index.html` — the main file

One self-contained page: ~29,000 lines, ~2.3 MB, ~60 views, the "Instrument" system. No
build step, no external runtime dependencies. Open it in a browser to run it.

## How to work in it

- **Never read the whole file.** Read the region you need. Locate first with `grep -n`,
  then read a bounded range.
- **Never pass replacement code through the shell.** Quotes get stripped silently and a
  syntax check still passes, so the damage surfaces later and far from its cause. Write
  the new text to a file, splice it in with a Node script, and **assert on unique anchor
  strings** — the script should refuse to write if an anchor matches zero times or more
  than once.
- **Scope every new CSS rule under `#fxRoot`** (or the owning view's id). `.dw-hd`,
  `.dw-tabs`, `.dw-body` and `.dw-foot` are shared class names between the Flux review
  workspace and the GL ledger inspector (`.acctsurf .gl-detail`); an unscoped selector
  restyles the other one.
- **Verify in the browser, not by reading.** Drive the view, assert the DOM, check the
  console. Geometry in this file is *measured* at runtime (`sizeDrawer`, `syncStick`), so
  reasoning about the CSS alone will mislead you.

## Structural facts worth knowing before you edit

- **THE PERIOD IS A PROPERTY OF THE BOOK, NOT A FILTER** (2026-09-01, owner). `BOOK.open` is
  the period that accepts work; `VIEW.period` is what you are looking at and defaults to it.
  Conflating them is the bug that gave one screen three different periods. The register lives
  in the SHELL because it is platform state — **the flux block is WRAPPED, so nothing declared
  in it is reachable from the close strip** (the first cut put it there and the strip threw on
  load). `KFX.syncPeriod(k)` is the only bridge and is called BY `setPeriod()`, never the
  reverse; `S.cur` is a cursor into the flux calendar, not a period of record, and cannot be
  seeded from `VIEW.period` because `S` is built before the shell runs.
- **`setPeriod()` is the ONE writer, and it must repaint the chrome itself** — `renderAll()`
  does not touch the close strip, which rides the nav paint cycle. Call `paintCloseStrip()`
  then `paintHdrCtl()`, in that order.
- **`canEditPeriod(sec)` is the guard every edit surface asks**, and it is SEPARATE from
  `caps()`: a Controller cannot edit a closed period, and an open period does not make you a
  reviewer. Both must hold. Absence from `BOOK.periods` reads as CLOSED — silence is not
  permission. `CLOSE_FUNCS` is the section vocabulary for locks.
- **Close/reopen is gated to Controller / CAO, confirmed in a PANEL, and recorded** in the
  period’s own history. Closing advances `BOOK.open` to the next month; closing the LAST
  period opens nothing and the whole platform goes read-only, which needs no special case.
  `setPeriodHard()` exists because `setPeriod()` short-circuits on an unchanged key — the
  reopen case, where the period is the same and its STATUS is not.
- **THE SELECTED PERIOD IS THE ACCOUNTING WORKSPACE'S CONTEXT, and pages INHERIT it.** Never
  write a period literal into a page: `periodCtxLine(tab)` states each page's own shape and
  `perLong` / `perEnd` / `perRange` / `perQtr` / `perWindow` / `perAdd` are the words. Month
  arithmetic is on the period KEY, never on `Date`. `glCtx()` is the reach — it is the subhead
  of every General Ledger page.
- **PERIOD is not PRESENTATION.** `F.periodType` is a WINDOW WIDTH (Monthly · Quarterly · YTD),
  not a period, and on Accounting it renders **only on Financials**, the one page whose figures
  it moves. A balance sheet scales nothing and drops the YTD pair — it answers "as of".
- **FLUX COMPARISONS ARE DETERMINISTIC** (`FX_VIEWS`): MoM · QoQ · YoY · YTD · 6M · 12M ·
  Budget · Forecast, each a NAMED PAIR of `grain` + `compare`, always anchored to the book's
  period. **No date picker, and never restore `custom`.** The view is DERIVED from the pair
  (`fxViewId()`), so saved views migrate on read and anything unknown resolves to MoM.
  `priorForView(v)` is the ONE prior rule: a window grain compares against the window
  immediately before it.
- **Trending is anchored** — `trendSeries()` is the single source for the chart, its statistics
  and the header line, and never runs past the selected period.
- **The Close workspace ORCHESTRATES; it never duplicates.** Each row reads the target
  workspace's own data and navigates to it — the period needs no argument because it is global.
  Do not put KPI cards back on it.
- **The period dropdown NAVIGATES ("View Jun 2026 Close →"); it does not close the ledger.**
  The ERP is the system of record for posting status. `closePeriod()`/`reopenPeriod()` live in
  the Close workspace header, gated by `PERIOD_CAPS`.
- **`PERIOD_CAPS` IS THE ONE TABLE for who may lock and unlock a period**, read by the close
  strip's menu AND by Settings' sign-off matrix. They were two literals and had already
  disagreed. A **Controller locks; only the Chief Accounting Officer reopens** — close and
  reopen are separate capabilities, so never gate `reopenPeriod()` on `canClosePeriod()`.
  A role absent from the table has neither.
- **The strip tag states the period's STATUS — `Open` / `Closed`, never the verb `Close`.**
  It read "CLOSE" beside an open period, which looked like a button and meant the opposite.
- **A gate that refuses the DEFAULT role must offer a way through.** `USER_ROLE` opens as
  Accounting Manager, which cannot close a period, so the menu carries `Act as Controller`
  (the prototype's own role switch, an OUTLINE so the commit stays the one filled control).
  It is not an access request and must not read as one.
- **`.ws-menu` IS A WHITE CONTENT-PLANE POPOVER** even though the ribbon opens it. Never use
  `--chrome-*` inside it: it rendered text at 1.17:1 on white, and the static gate cannot see
  it because those tokens are checked against chrome surfaces. A document handler also
  dismisses `#wsPop` on any click inside, so a button that REPLACES the menu body must call
  `event.stopPropagation()`; and set a WIDTH, not just a min-width, or a sentence stretches
  the menu to its own length.
- **`F.periodType` is a window WIDTH, not a period.** `windowMonths()` anchors itd/ytd/
  quarter/month to `VIEW.period`. `pickLens()` may set a width; it may NOT move the book.


- `<script id="egl-data">` is a **baked data snapshot**, not an import. It is serialised
  by `packages/core/tools/emit_enterprise_gl.mjs` and read by exactly one view,
  `view-egl`. This is the only bridge between `packages/` and the prototypes, and it is
  build-time and one-directional.
- The Flux review workspace renders at **two densities from one set of renderers**:
  `dwCtx()` derives every figure once per paint, `RWSEC` is a registry of named parts, and
  `RW_LAYOUT` is a pure composition table for everything except Overview. Overview is
  state-composed by `dwModeOf` / `dwOverviewKeys` (inspect / explain / review / locked).
  `RW_LAYOUT.drawer.sum` is the material-line default those keys return — not the only
  Overview. A part absent from `RWSEC` cannot render in either density. Do not fork it
  into two builders. Any refactor there must pass the byte-identity probe: hash
  `#fxDwBody.innerHTML` for every tab on every line before and after; they must be
  identical strings.
- **Four Overview modes, and a freeze.** inspect (`!r.req`) reads — drivers, a quiet
  optional-explanation cue, no footer. explain (`r.req`, draft/returned) works — the
  explanation owns Save/Accept; the footer is Submit once coverage clears. review
  (`submitted`) and locked (`approved`) report. Submitted reads exactly like locked in
  the explanation card (`canPrep = !lock && !frozen && caps().prepare`): a preparer
  must not rewrite the words a reviewer is reading. The way back is the reviewer's
  Return, a recorded transition — name the freeze rather than silently dropping Edit.
  Every mode's Overview ends with a common tail — Workflow & review, Evidence, Comments
  (`dwOverviewKeys` appends `['reviewer','evidRow','cmtPeek']`) — each a collapsed
  summary with a chevron to its own tab, mirroring the `apps/review` inspector. The
  header is a **four-figure band** — current · compared · variance · Δ%, equal billing,
  colour on the two variance cells only (2026-08-29; this replaced the hero, and the
  contribution bars went with it). **Key drivers is a list that FOOTS** to the header's
  variance: the residual is NOT a driver (different axis, same total) and the remainder
  row absorbs rounding so the PRINTED column adds up.
  **Every section is a full-width band on a sheet**, in the dock, on the full page and
  in the Korvyn Assistant — one rail at `--s-4` for header, tabs, band titles and rows
  alike. This supersedes the "white hairline blocks / elevated column cards" treatment.
  Traps: compare content to the HEADER, never cards to other cards (a uniformly wrong
  stack is uniformly symmetric); cap the PROSE, never the card (`.rw-col.solo` capped at
  120ch left every solo tab ~500px short); and a scrollbar takes layout width, so
  `scrollbar-gutter:stable` plus a runtime-measured `--dw-sbw` / `--cp-sbw` right margin
  on the chrome is what keeps every band on one edge. A derived classification
  pill (Routine / Material · explained / Needs review) and a reviewer initials
  avatar sit on the Explanation and Workflow blocks respectively.
- **Flux filters are inline and TABBED BY CATEGORY, not a drawer** (2026-08-28, owner's
  direction). The shared `#filterbar` drawer is hidden on Flux (`paintTopbar()` gates on
  `fxHere`); filters live in `fxFilterFields()` → `.fx-filters` inside `#fxRoot`, as a tab
  strip over ONE contextual row: `.fx-filters` = `.fx-ftabs` + `.fx-fields`, nothing else.
  `FX_FTABS` = Period · Scope · Workflow · Variance · Basis · Display · Saved Views · Actions;
  the last three are `cfg:1` (configure, not filter — never counted). Filter fields are a fixed
  **180px × 28px × 6px radius on every tab** — pin the 6px explicitly, never inherit
  `var(--radius)`, because `body.fx-mercury #fxRoot.aw` raises it to 14px and turns a 28px
  control into a capsule (same trap for `.fx-fields .seg`); long values ellipsise and the
  tooltip carries `label · value`. **There is no second
  toolbar and there must not be one**: `pageActions()`/`viewControls()` are retired, Display
  owns mode + Table. **Two display modes only** — `stmt` (Statement) and `full` (Narrative);
  Review and Financials merged once they differed by a single column. Every retired key
  (`status`, `nums`, `compact`, `review`, `cmt`) migrates on READ in `dispMode()` and anything
  unrecognised falls back to Statement, never Narrative. The keyboard legend and handler both
  derive from `DISP_MODES.length` — do not hard-code `1–3`. Display Saved Views owns the star + Views menu, Actions owns the ellipsis.
  Rejected placements: above the tabs, below the fields, and the shell topbar. **Workflow**
  not "Review" (Review is a display mode); **Variance** not "Movement". Submit is the one CTA
  and renders ONLY when lines are ready, stating the count ("Submit 3 ready lines") — the
  count and `submitAll()` share `readyToSubmit()` so they can never disagree. It stays on the
  STATEMENT, never in the review panel: it is a bulk action over the current view, while the
  per-line Submit already lives in the panel footer (`exSubmit`), gated on coverage. Property and
  Cost center sit behind **More** (`FX_MOREDIMS`) but still count against Scope. Do not add
  controls the engine cannot act on (currency, owner, GAAP basis, movement type were specified
  and deliberately omitted). The review panel is a **full-height dock** in the Korvyn
  assistant's band: `sizeDrawer()` pins it below whatever occupies its column, so the
  full-width rows reserve that column instead — `.fx-work`/`.fx-focusline` at
  `--fx-dw-w + 12px`, `.topbar`/`.secnav`/`.subnav` at `+48px` (wider container). Declare
  these, never compute them from the drawer's own left edge — that is a feedback loop and the
  panel walks off-screen. `body.fx-mercury #fxRoot .fx-work` sets a later `margin` shorthand,
  so scope the reservation `#fxRoot.aw`; keep `.fxt` `white-space:nowrap`. **Full screen
  (`S.dwPage`) SWAPS per tab, it does not scroll** — `dwPageCols()` reads `RW_LAYOUT.drawer`
  so one registry drives both densities; Overview keeps the 3-column argument. All page chrome
  hides under `body.fx-dw-page`; the statement tabs need `!important` (paintSubnav sets
  `display` inline), and the class must be gated on `#fxRoot.offsetParent!==null` or it leaks
  to other pages. **That same `body.fx-mercury #fxRoot .fx-work` block also beat the page's
  own `#fxRoot.dw-page>.fx-work{display:none}`** (one id + two classes + an element vs one id
  + two classes, and later in the sheet), so the worklist tiles stayed on screen through full
  screen — scope the mercury restyle `#fxRoot:not(.dw-page)`, never `!important` the
  stand-down.
- **Overview means the same thing at both densities** (2026-08-29, later). `RW_LAYOUT.page` is
  the reference's FIVE — judge `explain`·`attn`, comp `drivers`·`evidRow`, rec
  `reviewer`·`cmtPeek` — not the full contents of four other tabs (it used to mount 13 regions
  against the dock's 5, so `evid`/`drill`/`prior`/`trend`/`trail`/the thread were each
  reachable two ways). `attn` is the one page-only addition, because the itemised blockers need
  room the dock has not. `evidRow`/`cmtPeek` are SUMMARIES with a chevron, never a second copy.
  **Do not re-mount a tab's full contents into Overview**, and do not give a part an
  `if(X.page)return ''` guard — `drivers()` had one, so Key drivers did not exist on the page
  and the untitled `drill` stood in its place.
- **Comments follow the explanation they answer** (2026-08-29, owner's direction). `cmtPeek`
  is SECOND in Overview at both densities (`['explain','cmtPeek','drivers','reviewer','evidRow']`;
  page `judge:['explain','cmtPeek','attn']`) and carries the last message plus `cmtComposer()`.
  **PEEK AND REPLY, NEVER THE WHOLE THREAD** — the Comments tab is 346px empty and 789px with
  three messages, which pushes Workflow & review to y≈1150 in a 572px dock body; the peek costs
  67→145px and moves it 441→586. The archive, filters, threaded replies and the awaiting worklist
  stay on the tab. This knowingly trades away the reference's card order (…Workflow & review ·
  Evidence · Comments) — **if it is reversed, reverse the ORDER, not the composer.** The peek and
  `cmtComp` both emit `#cmtIn` and are never on screen together; while `S.cmtReplyTo` is set the
  peek renders `.rw-peekaim` (a line naming where the reply is, with a way back) rather than
  standing down silently the way `cmtComp` can — `cmtComp`'s replacement is visible three rows
  above it, the peek's is on another tab.
- **One tab order, one badge rule.** `FX_DOCKTABS` and `FX_PAGETABS` are declared as data
  beside each other and both read one `badgeOf()`; the four shared tabs keep their relative
  order (Overview · Comments · Evidence · History) at both densities, with the two page-only
  analytical tabs straight after Overview. Two hand-written literals with two badge rules
  inside them is what let the orders and the counts diverge. `--rule`/`--rule-strong` are redeclared in the dark block — ramp-derived
  aliases at `:root` otherwise substitute the LIGHT border and every dark hairline vanishes.
  `#tbScope` (the topbar entity tree) is **hidden on Flux** — it
  filters nothing here (`sliceOk()` never reads `F.entity`) and lists a different entity
  vocabulary than the Scope tab, so it could contradict the scope actually in force; hidden
  only on Flux, untouched elsewhere. `glHead()`/`periodNav()` no longer run on Flux: the header
  restated what the tabs state, and the month stepper became the Period field. ONE popover
  system (`#pop`) for every selector — anchored, never modal, one at a time, Escape/outside
  closes. Other pages keep the drawer — do not delete it.
  **Each tab's live-filter count is load-bearing**: tabs hide filters, and
  a signed statement must never be filtered for an invisible reason — do not drop the counts
  without replacing that guarantee.

- **EVERY FILTER COMMITS ON CLICK** (2026-08-31, owner's direction). There is no staged
  Apply anywhere in the ribbon; `popDraft`/`popDraftX`, `popApply()` and the
  `.pop-ft` footer are retired. A multi-select writes straight to `S` and calls
  `popLive()`, which re-renders and keeps the menu open, anchored and focused on the row
  you were on. The menu renders from `S`, which is what the statement renders from, so the
  two cannot disagree. **Do not reintroduce a draft.** Select all acts on the
  search-visible options only; Clear lives in the header, like every other menu.
  ONE MENU ANATOMY: `pop-h → [pop-s, only when >12 options] → pop-l`, every row clickable.
  Width is 300px; only the dimension multi-selects take `.wide` (340px), because they carry
  a second column of figures.
- **A LIST APPLIES ON CLICK; A FORM APPLIES ON APPLY.** The explanation rule is the only
  form and the only staged control: you cannot half-type `0.25` without passing through
  `0`, and a statement recomputing per keystroke would flag every line on the way. Its
  footer is Save as default · Cancel · Apply. Everything else is a list and commits at once.
- **ONE SELECTION AFFORDANCE** (2026-08-31, owner). This REVERSES "single-select is a tick,
  multi-select is a checkbox": that rule produced a bare tick + a filled `--accent-bg` row on
  one menu and a square box + a neutral row on the next. Every option row carries the same
  14px box and **no row fill** — the box is the selected state. `.pop-o.ck` still carries the
  checkbox ROLE in `popEnhance()`, so the a11y distinction survives without a visual one.
  A row that cannot hold a box (More, a saved view, a Table-menu command) is tagged `.nobox`
  by `popEnhance()` and carries its state on the row — never hand-written at a call site.
- **The worklist bar READS FROM YOUR SIDE OF THE REVIEW** (2026-08-31, owner). One pipeline,
  three lenses, five tiles: preparer `needs explanation → ready to submit → with reviewer`
  reviewer `awaiting your review · you returned · with preparer`, read-only the same in
  neutral words with no hero. Then `explained%` + `reviewed N/M`.
  **It cannot flip on the job title** — `FX_CAPS` gives Manager/Controller/CAO BOTH caps, so
  `lensOf()` maps caps to a lens and a DUAL role gets the preparer pipeline (its reviewer
  pipeline is three zeros by construction: `awaitingMyReview` excludes lines you prepared).
  That role's review queue surfaces as an **exception tile** when non-empty. **A
  Preparing|Reviewing switch was built and REMOVED by the owner** — the lens is derived,
  there is no `S.lens` and nothing to press.
  **`rowReady(r)` is ONE predicate with three readers** — `readyToSubmit()`, the
  `Ready to submit` filter and the tile — so the Submit CTA, the filter and the count can
  never disagree.
  **A TILE OR FILTER THAT NAMES A REVIEW STATE MUST READ `fxStateOf(r)`**, never re-derive
  it. The `needs explanation` tile and the `open` filter each tested `!r.clear` and so
  dropped a material line whose movement is small enough to sit inside tolerance with no
  explanation written — the threshold/tolerance conflation. `r.req` says an explanation is
  REQUIRED; `r.clear` says an existing one is SUFFICIENT. Different questions.
  **The hero says `needs explanation`** — the same words `FXST.need`, the Status filter and
  the chip use; do not invent a fourth. Tiles are `flex:1 1 auto`, NOT `1 1 0`: equal widths
  made the longest label spill its tile once the review panel took 440px.
- **The worklist speaks the review's own words** (2026-08-31, owner):
  **"need you" was retired because it was untrue** — it counts every required line not yet
  signed off, whoever holds it, so nothing in it is about you. **"signed off" → "reviewed"**
  to match `FXST.done`; do not let the bar and the Explanation column name one state twice.
  **flagged** was dropped as a duplicate (`flag` == the first pipeline tile at the start of a close, and
  the population is already stated by the explanation rule's caption) and **Ties to TB** as a
  control rather than a work item — `paintMethod()` and the memo still state it, `tieOut()`
  is unchanged. An exception tile still renders when it is the ACTIVE filter, and the
  Selected filters chips are the general off-switch that made those removals safe.
- **The 12-period trend column is a LEVEL LINE and stays one.** A horizontal bar was built
  twice and rejected twice (2026-08-31, owner): eleven change bars, then a single diverging
  bar. **A single bar of the current movement restates the Δ Amount column** — same number,
  same sign, same green/red, two columns away — and the only thing it added, "which line
  moved most", is answered better by sorting on the Δ Amount header. Its shared scale also
  made the longest bar a SUBTOTAL. **The column’s only non-redundant job is CONTEXT** — is
  this movement normal for this line — which needs history, which is what the line carries
  and one bar cannot. If a MoM/QoQ basis is wanted, put it on a control that shows a SERIES.
- **The Δ pair sits under one CHANGE span** (2026-08-31, from the comps). Two header rows.
  **The span is CONDITIONAL and `fxShed()` syncs its colspan** — a colspan is an attribute,
  the one thing a class cannot hide, so a span left at 2 over a shed Δ% drags every heading
  to its right out of line. **One token drives both rows**: the sub-row pins at
  `--fx-stick + --fx-hgrp-h` and the group cell (the only row-1 cell without `rowspan="2"`)
  is pinned to that same token — guess the height and the rows overlap and sit over the body.
- **THE INCOME STATEMENT IS PUBLISHED: costs POSITIVE** (2026-08-31, owner). The engine stays
  additive — `lineRaw()` keeps natural signs and runs the subtotal recursion; `lineVal()` is
  `lineRaw × lineSgn()` and is what every other caller reads. A line is tagged `exp:1`;
  NEVER infer it from the section (Gain on disposition sits among costs and is a credit).
  The balance sheet is untouched. **`byDim()`/`rwAgg()` bypass `lineVal`** and sum raw slices,
  so they carry `lineSgn` too — without it the drivers stop footing to the header variance.
  Account sub-rows use `acctSgn` (resolved through `COA.c`).
- **COLOUR FOLLOWS FAVOURABILITY, THE ARROW FOLLOWS THE SIGN.** `unfav()` used to be `r.d<0`
  on both of its branches and worked only because costs were negative; it is stated properly
  now (`ln.exp ? d>0 : d<0`). Component amounts use `unfavOn(id,d)`. Tying the ARROW to
  favourability was tried and reverted: an up-arrow then meant "good", so a cost that FELL
  rendered as a green up-arrow.
- **`colHead()` names the HEADER, `colName()` names the COLUMN.** The header carries the
  period (`Jun 2026`, `Q2 2026`, or `Budget` via `cmpBtnLabel()`); the picker, the sort
  tooltip and the shed order keep `Current`/`Compared`, because "turn off Jun 2026" is
  meaningless in a column list and would reword every month.
- **`S.cols` and `COLS_DEFAULT` are two statements of ONE default and must agree.** They had
  drifted (trend was in the reset set and not the opening set), so "Reset to default" landed
  somewhere a session never opens on. `trend`, `cmts` and `supp` are OPTIONS: in `COL_DEFS`,
  one click away in Display > Table > Columns, and in neither default.
- **`cmts` / `supp` are INVENTORY columns** (rule 12): quiet grey numeral + icon, never a
  filled pill, and an em dash rather than `0`. Support counts `evidenceDocs()` (deduped by
  file name, the list the Evidence tab reads), never `evidenceFor()`, which counts citations.
- **`RWSEC.drivers` (`.rw-kd`) IS ORPHANED** — `dwOverviewKeys()` has not returned `drivers`
  since 2026-08-30 and `RW_LAYOUT.page` is the two-column desk. Swept: 6 tabs × 2 densities ×
  6 lines, zero hits. The LIVE drivers view is `dwDrivers()`. Do not "fix" the orphan and
  think you changed the screen.
- **`shareOf(v,tot)` is the ONE gate on printing a share.** A percentage is only meaningful
  while its denominator can carry one: below half a display unit the total rounds to 0.0 on
  screen and the share explodes (measured 23474% on a line that moved 0.0 — the `contrib`
  fault, surviving in the one place a share is still printed). It returns null there and the
  amounts carry the decomposition alone.
- **`colOn()` inserts in `COL_DEFS` order, never appends** — appending put every newly
  enabled column after Explanation, which is the widest and only text column and belongs
  last. Manual `colMove` order is preserved; this decides only where a NEW column lands.
  `colName()` is the one namer, so the header and the picker agree.
- **A CUSTOM PROPERTY RESOLVES WHERE IT IS DECLARED.** `--pos`/`--neg`/`--warn`/`--neu` were
  declared only in `:root` as `var(--success)` and friends, so they froze the LIGHT value and
  inherited it into dark mode — every green/red figure in the app rendered ~2:1 on the dark
  surface. The `[data-theme="dark"]` block’s own comment states the rule ("if you add a
  ramp-derived alias to `:root`, add it here too") and it had been applied to `--pos-ink`
  alone. Re-declare the alias AND anything it derives from (`--neutral`) in that block.
  **The static contrast gate cannot see this** — it resolves `--pos` analytically and gets the
  dark value; only computed style in a browser shows the freeze.
- **Δ% IS NEUTRAL ON PURPOSE** (rule 9: colour at most two columns). Colour lands on the Δ
  amount and on the F/U mark beside the percentage — the same green/red — and a third
  rendering of one signal is what colouring the number would be. On an income statement the
  sign and the favourability are different facts (an expense that ROSE is a negative Δ and
  unfavourable); the letter carries that, the percentage is a magnitude and stays quiet.
  **A per-line progress/coverage BAR COLUMN has been declined twice**: `contrib` was exactly
  that and was retired — 25 bars are a second chart competing with the figures, and a per-row
  scale cannot be read across rows. Coverage is a one-line question; the inspect panel
  answers it against the tolerance tick.
- **SELECTED FILTERS is a slim strip under the field row** (2026-08-31, owner). The eight
  tabs stay; `fxSelStrip()` states what is on as `Name: value ×` chips. **It does not render
  when nothing is selected** (zero height, not an empty band), it is ramp not accent (the
  strip is where you read, the fields are where you act), and **it survives the fold** —
  collapsing may hide which CONTROLS exist, never WHAT is filtering a statement whose
  numbers get signed. A chip's body opens the owning tab and that filter's menu (anchored
  to the FIELD, not the chip); its × removes just that filter. Both are real buttons.
  `fxTabOf()` derives the tab from `FX_FTABS`, never a second list.
  **`fxFilterState(k)` is the ONE statement of what a filter reads** — the field and its
  chip both call it, so they cannot drift; the exclusion wording ("Excl. 2") lives there.
  `setQuery()` calls `fxPaintSel()` so the strip repaints without rebuilding the row that
  holds the search input.
  *A wholesale replacement of the tab surface with a chip bar was built and REJECTED by the
  owner — the defect was never the tabs, it was that nothing stated what was selected. Do
  not retire the tabs.*
- **NO DESCRIPTION AT THE BOTTOM OF A FILTER, AND NONE ON A ROW** (2026-08-31, owner). Nine
  `.pop-b` footnotes explaining the control above them are gone; so are the row subs that
  restated an option's own name. **The sub column carries a VALUE, never a sentence** — a
  resolved period, a movement figure, a count. The two surviving captions are figures: the
  rule editor's impact count and the 12-period trend readout.
- **`popPlace()` calls `popRebind()` first, and this is load-bearing.** `renderAll()`
  rebuilds the field row and DESTROYS the trigger `#pop` is anchored to; measuring a
  detached node gives a zero rect and parks the menu at (8,6). The guard belongs where the
  rectangle is read, not in each commit path — putting it in `popLive()` alone missed
  `setReason`, `setSort`, `setDens` and `setFullAll`. When the trigger cannot
  be recovered the menu stays put rather than being placed against a zero rect.
- `S.excl[dim]` is a REAL filter through `dimOk()` in `sliceOk()`, not a label — it rides
  in `snapshot()` beside the dimension arrays, and every scope renderer must spell the
  exclusion ("Excl. 2", "All except 2 entities"), because the same list under the flag
  means the opposite thing. An empty selection always clears the flag. It is surfaced as
  the `.pop-sw` **Only these | All except** switch, which appears only once something is
  chosen — there is no reading of an empty set.
- **The explanation rule is FOUR CONTROLS and no names** (2026-08-31, owner's mockup):
  Amount · Percent · Logic (Either|Both) · Residual tolerance, in `S.mat`, with `S.matDef`
  as what "Save as default" writes and what clearing returns to. The named profiles are
  gone — `Monthly close policy` / `SEC / MD&A` / `Tight review` were three presets over the
  same four numbers, and "SEC / MD&A" claimed a reporting basis this model does not carry.
  **`prof()` is the ONE place that phrases the rule** (`prof().nm === matRule()`), so the
  memo, the chips, the panel and the trail cannot drift; do not reintroduce a policy NAME
  without a record behind it.
  The `caps().policy` gate stays and `matCommit()` logs before/after: moving a bar mid-close
  un-flags a breaching line and the flux memo then prints the new rule as though it had been
  in force all period. **Do not remove the gate to make the form feel lighter.**
  `matCap()` refreshes the impact caption WITHOUT repainting the form — a repaint takes the
  caret, the same contract `cmtField()`/`exField()` hold.
- Materiality thresholds live in $M and convert once through `matFac()`. `floor` is a
  constant, not a control — it is the point below which two tiny balances are not worth
  comparing, not a threshold a reviewer sets.
- **`S.thresh` is in DISPLAY units**, so `setUnits()` must re-express it. Left alone,
  millions → thousands turned a 1.0M floor into a 1.0K one. Materiality is stored in $M and
  converted once through `matFac()`; the ad-hoc threshold is the one that needs converting.
- **The explanation and the comments are ONE conversation** (2026-08-28, owner's direction).
  Every voice uses one grammar, `.rw-msg`: avatar in the gutter, identity on one line, words
  against a rail. The explanation is the lead statement (`.lead`); a Korvyn draft wears indigo
  `--ai`, never the cobalt accent; your own messages take `.mine`; awaited voices are hollow
  silhouettes (`.owed`); the composer is the last message (`.comp`), not the first. Order is
  load-bearing — thread, then composer, then who has not answered. `cmtField()` must never
  repaint (it toggles `.live` and autosizes in place, or the caret is lost). "Awaiting a
  comment" stays collapsed behind `S.cmtOwedOpen` with every action intact. **Replies are ONE
  level deep by design**: every message has a stable id (`k:<key>` for a lineage contribution,
  `id` for a discussion comment) and a reply stores `parent`; `cmtRootOf()` re-parents in
  `cmtSave()`, so the STORED shape can never exceed two levels. The reply box renders inside
  its group (`cmtComp` stands down while `S.cmtReplyTo` is set); the kind filter applies to
  ROOTS only, or replies orphan; the draft is discarded on aim/un-aim; and `S.cmtReplyTo` must
  clear in `pick()`, `pickAt()` and `closeDrawer()`. `cmtComp` is a
  registry part: add it to BOTH `RW_LAYOUT.drawer.comments` and `RW_LAYOUT.page.rec`. The base
  avatar rule is `#fxRoot.aw .fx-detail .rw-av`, so `.rw-av.ai` must match that specificity.
- **The thread is a TIMELINE** (2026-08-28). ONE connector down the gutter
  (`.rw-msg::before`) behind opaque badges — never a per-message rail beside the prose,
  which is box-in-box applied to strokes. `.rw-thread`/`.rw-grp` are `gap:0`; spacing is
  padding, or the connector is cut between every pair. The VOICE lives on the badge: a
  person is ramp, YOU are the accent, a Korvyn draft is indigo (`--ai`, never a second UI
  accent), an awaited voice is a dashed hollow circle. `.who` is `flex:0 0 100%` with role
  and time on a quiet line beneath it. Rows are full-bleed via `--rw-bleed` set on
  `.dw-body`; hover is `--surface2` and the replied-to message takes `.aim` (`--accent-bg`).

- A **materiality threshold** (`r.req`) decides whether a line must be explained at all. A
  **tolerance** (`r.clear`) decides whether an explanation covers enough of the movement.
  They are different numbers — conflating them is how a line below the threshold ends up
  permanently unsubmittable.
- Roles resolve at call time from `USER_ROLE` through `FX_CAPS`, keyed on
  `Accounting Manager` / `Controller` / `Chief Accounting Officer` / `Asset Manager` /
  `FP&A Analyst` / `External Auditor`. An unknown role falls back to view-only.
  Segregation of duties is enforced at the action (`canReviewLine`), not in the UI.

## Corporate Flux — page shell (2026-08-28, UI/UX Phase 1)

Flux is the reference implementation for the Korvyn-wide interaction language. Zendesk is
the reference for *discipline* only — never branding, never IA. The shell (dark ribbon,
module nav, Accounting rail, Search / Ask Korvyn, notifications, profile, scope) is
untouched.

- **The page header is the shell's title row**, written by `paintTopbar()`: the name
  **Corporate Flux**, then `KFX.pageContext()` — *"Jun 2026 vs May 2026 · Consolidated ·
  USD"*. Every clause comes from the function the matching control reads, so the header
  and the Period / Scope / Basis fields cannot drift. **Never add a header inside
  `#fxRoot`** — that is why `glHead()` was retired; the page name would be on screen
  twice.
- The stack is **A: what view you are in → B: how it is filtered**: title + context →
  statement tabs (`.ktabs`, level 1, accent) → command tabs (`.ktabs.lvl2`, ink) → the
  one contextual field row → the statement.
- Tabs and dropdowns come from the shared `.ktabs` / `.pop` primitives — see
  `design-system.mdc`. Do not add Flux-specific tab or menu styling.
- **The accent marks the OPEN field, not the filtered one.** A narrowing filter reads
  through its value (`--hint` → `--ink` 500 + stronger border). The "is anything
  filtering this?" guarantee is the per-tab counts, which are load-bearing: they are
  looked up by `data-tab` in `syncFilterChrome()` and refreshed together.
- **The field row never wraps** (`flex-wrap:nowrap` + scroll). With the review panel open
  it is 684px against 932px of content; wrapping made it two rows and pushed the
  statement 36px down.
- `#fxRoot.aw` must not redefine `--radius`.
- Phase 1 deliberately did NOT touch: the statement table's columns, variance treatment,
  row hierarchy or account groups; the comments/support panel; the narrative. The
  eight-tab command architecture and its names are unchanged. The table's *frame* and
  filled header band are surface treatment, not a redesign of the statement.
- **Boxy (2026-08-28):** `#fxRoot.aw` is a GROUND — no background, no border, no radius,
  `padding:0`. The command surface, the worklist tiles and the statement are each their
  own bordered white card on it. See `design-system.mdc`.
- **Never put `overflow:hidden` on `.card.fx-table`.** The column heads are
  `position:sticky; top:var(--fx-stick)`; an `overflow:hidden` ancestor becomes their
  containing block and drops the header ~147px into the middle of the statement.
  `#fxRoot .fx-table{overflow:visible}` exists for this — do not out-specify it.
- The sticky `thead` fills with `--n-50`, one step lighter than the `--bg2` section
  rows, so the two filled bands never read as one. It must not use `var(--bg)` — that
  borrows the page colour for a band inside a white card.

## Checks

```bash
node tools/check_chrome_themes.mjs
node tools/check_text_contrast.mjs    # index.html CONTENT text passes WCAG AA (both modes)
node tools/check_spacing_scale.mjs     # index.html spacing stays on the 4px scale (ratchet)
```

Then the browser sweep: open the file, drive every tab, assert each view rendered, console
clean.
